@page "/docs/common/data-api"

@* Data from an API: a provider in the browser sending its DataRequest to your own API or an OData
   endpoint, the filter's text form, and what the server enforces (export files are in Downloads.razor). Split from Data and filters
   (Data.razor), which covers the request, fields, filters in code, operators, case and dates. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="data-api">
    <Lead>
        In a WebAssembly or Auto app, the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> runs in the browser, so it sends its
        <Code>DataRequest</Code> to an API. The request can go in a GET's URL, as JSON, or as OData query options. On
        your own server, the request can use only the fields you declare.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Calling your own API</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The client puts the request in a GET's URL with <Code>ToQueryString()</Code>. The server reads it back with
            <Code>DataRequest&lt;T&gt;.Parse</Code>, and <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> applies it to the query through the
            fields you declare. Both sides use the same row type. Here it's <Code>OrderRow</Code>, in a project that the
            client and the server share:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Orders.razor (client)">@Client</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)" HighlightLines="8,11">@Server</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Unescaped, the request in the URL looks like
            <Code>sort=-placed&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code>. It also contains <Code>total=false</Code>
            when the component doesn't need the count, and <Code>parent</Code> and <Code>branch</Code> for a TreeList. To
            send the request in a POST instead, serialize it with <Code>System.Text.Json</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The request names fields by the client's column keys, so the server's fields need the same keys. With the same
            row type, they match. A column on a path, such as <Code>o =&gt; o.Customer.Name</Code>, has the key
            <Code>customer.name</Code>. Set its <Code>SortKey</Code> to the server field's key. For rows read by
            <Code>DynamicColumn</Code>, build the server's fields with <Code>DataFields.ByName</Code>, by the same names and
            types. See <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#fields">Fields</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A DataGrid's <Code>GroupsProvider</Code> sends a <Code>DataGroupRequest</Code>, and a column's
            <Code>FilterValues</Code> sends a <Code>FilterValuesRequest</Code>. Send both the same way: write each with
            <Code>ToQueryString()</Code>, and read it on the server with <Code>Parse</Code>. Answer a group request with
            <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> through the same fields. Answer a value list with
            <Code>DataFields.LoadValuesAsync</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Filters as text</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A filter can be written as text, in a subset of OData's <Code>$filter</Code>. The URL uses this form for
            <Code>filter=</Code>. A filter's <Code>ToString()</Code> writes the text, and <Code>Filter.Parse</Code> reads it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Syntax</Code>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Write</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>eq ne gt ge lt le</code></td><td>Comparisons. <code>eq null</code> and <code>ne null</code> are the null checks.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>status in ('Paid','Pending')</code></td><td>Any of the values. <code>not (status in (...))</code> matches none of them.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>customer contains 'ada'</code></td><td>Text that contains the value. <code>contains(customer,'ada')</code> also works. So do <code>startswith</code> and <code>endswith</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>between(total,100,500)</code></td><td>A range. To leave an end without a limit, set it to <code>null</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>isempty(email)</code></td><td>No value, or empty text. <code>isnotempty</code> is the opposite.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>date(placed) eq 2026-10-28</code></td><td>On that day.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>and or not ( )</code></td><td>Groups. <code>and</code> is evaluated before <code>or</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>'O''Brien'</code>, <code>-1.5</code>, <code>true</code>, <code>2026-10-28T09:30:00Z</code></td><td>Values: text in single quotes (with a quote doubled), numbers, true or false, dates and times, and Guids.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <TextBlock>
            Parsing only builds the filter. Names are always field keys, and values are always values. So
            <Code>name eq 'x'' or 1 eq 1'</Code> looks for that exact text.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Text that isn't in this form doesn't parse, and <Code>TryParse</Code> returns false.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A request read from a URL with such a filter matches nothing. A <Code>skip</Code> or <Code>take</Code> that doesn't parse is ignored.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Text comparisons ignore case here too, unlike OData's own <Code>contains</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                The text form doesn't write <Code>MatchCase</Code>, so a condition sent this way ignores case on the server. To
                keep <Code>MatchCase</Code>, send the request as JSON. See
                <Anchor Href="docs/common/data#text-and-case">Text and case</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">OData endpoints</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            For an OData endpoint, <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code> writes the request as OData query options:
            <Code>$filter</Code>, <Code>$orderby</Code>, <Code>$skip</Code>, <Code>$top</Code>, <Code>$count</Code> and
            <Code>$search</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@OData</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                OData's text comparisons match case. To ignore case, both sides are written lower-cased:
                <Code>contains(tolower(customer),'ada')</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>Field paths use <Code>/</Code>: <Code>customer/name</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Values are written in their fields' types. A Table's request contains its fields. For a request you read with
                <Code>Parse</Code>, pass the fields: <Code>ToODataQuery(fields)</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Keys are lower case. If the service's property names are case-sensitive, set each column's
                <Code>SortKey</Code> to the service's name.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>OData has no cursor, so pass <Code>request.Cursor</Code> to the service yourself.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Limits and security</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A request that comes from the browser can ask for anything, so the server decides what it can reach. These rules
            apply when you pass your own fields to <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> or <Code>Apply</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Only declared fields can be reached.</strong> A condition on a key that isn't a filterable field matches
                nothing, and a sort on one is ignored. <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property.
                So call it on a type made for the API, not on an entity that has a password hash.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Cap the range.</strong> The request sets its own <Code>Take</Code>, or asks for every row when it has
                none.
                The server example above allows at most 100 rows.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Large or deep filters match nothing.</strong> A filter with more than 1,000 nodes (conditions, groups
                and nots), or nested more than 64 deep, matches no rows. The text form nests at most 32 deep.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor>: the request and result, fields, filters in code and the operators.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access">Data access by render mode</Anchor>: one interface for pages on the server and in the browser.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes#pages-in-the-browser">Pages in the browser</Anchor>: what changes when components run in the browser.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/downloads">Downloads</Anchor>: a grid's rows as Excel, CSV, PDF or a format of your own, from the same request.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Syntax = """
        total ge 100 and (status in ('Paid','Pending') or customer contains 'ada')
        """;

    private const string Client = """
        @inject HttpClient Api

        <Table TItem="OrderRow" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="20">
            <Columns>
                <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable />
                <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable Filterable />
    
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<OrderRow>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<OrderRow> request) =>
                (await Api.GetFromJsonAsync<DataResult<OrderRow>>(
                    $"api/orders?{request.ToQueryString()}", request.CancellationToken))!;
        }
        """;

    private const string Server = """
        using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;   // CountAsync, ToListAsync

        app.MapGet("/api/orders", async (HttpContext context, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var request = DataRequest<OrderRow>.Parse(context.Request.QueryString.Value ?? "")
                with { CancellationToken = context.RequestAborted };
            // At most 100 rows.
            request = request with { Take = Math.Min(request.Take ?? 100, 100) };
            var rows = db.Orders.AsNoTracking()   // an initializer: EF Core filters and sorts on it
                .Select(o => new OrderRow { Id = o.Id, Customer = o.Customer.Name, Total = o.Total });
            return await request.ToResultAsync(rows, DataFields.Of<OrderRow>(),
                count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct), list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
        });

        public sealed record OrderRow
        {
            public int Id { get; init; }
            public string Customer { get; init; } = "";
            public decimal Total { get; init; }
        }
        """;

    private const string OData = """
        var url = $"odata/Orders?{request.ToODataQuery()}";
        // Unescaped:
        // $filter=contains(tolower(customer),'ada') and total ge 100
        // &$orderby=placed desc&$skip=20&$top=10&$count=true
        """;
}
